answers.body:
You can use omp tasks: int main() {
#pragma omp parallel
  {
#pragma omp single
    {
#pragma omp task
      increment1(300000000);
#pragma omp task // if you leave out this pragma the printf will be executed 
                 // by the same thread that created the increment1 task
                 // otherwise it can be scheduled by any OMP thread
      printf("continue\n");

#pragma omp taskwait
    }
  }
  return 0;
} This example will create two tasks (one for the increment1 function and one for printf("continue\n") which will be distributed among the OMP threads. The tasks might be scheduled in any order, but when there are more OMP threads than tasks they will be scheduled concurrently. Additional note: The #pragma omp single directive is there so that the tasks are only created by one thread. other wise each thread would create a task for increment1 and the printf statement.

