} when you leave braces out only the next statement after the "for" statement is executed in the loop, so the "matrix2" line is executed after the loops end, at which point i and j are out of bound, therefore (double*) matrix2[i] is rubbish; accessing matrix2[i] may or may not cause a segfault depending on the heap layout, but accessing matrix2[i][j] most likely will since there's no telling where it's going. That's not the only problem: matrix[i] is allocated as a pointer to an array of sizeof(double) * size, while you're accessing matrix[i][size*size - 1] in the loop. In C-derived languages, leaving out the braces for "if", "do", "for", "case" and "while" statements usually is a bad idea; imagine having to add more lines, it can get bothersome. It's hard to read and reason about as well when the code gets more complicated.

