answers.body:
As the syntax for a critical construct in OpenMP is: #pragma omp critical [(name)] new-line
structured-block the only way I see to have the operations performed in an initializer list of a constructor critical is to define them critical inside the class being initialized. To better explain myself: class Base {
public:
  Base() {
#pragma omp critical
    {
      /* Some critical operation here */
    }
  }

  /* ... */
}

class Derived : public Base {
public:
  Derived() : Base() {} // Here base call some critical code

  /* ... */
} If there is a need to make the operations in Base constructor critical only in a few objects, I would personally try to devise two different Base policies and restructure Derived to be an host class (see chapter 1 of "Modern C++ Design" for a reference on policies).
The reasoning remains obviously the same if aggregation, rather than inheritance, is taken into consideration.

