OpenMP has an overhead which adds a constant term to the time calculation.  Let me give an example. Let's assume your algorithm finishes in A*n where A is some constant and n is the number of items you will iterate over.  Let's also assume that your algorithm parallelizes perfectly so that if you have k threads the parallelized algorithm finishes in O(n)/k time.  Due to the OpenMP overhead the time to run will be A*n/k + B where B is the overhead.  Therefore, in order for you to see any benfit from OpenMP A*n/k + B < A*n .  For some range of values of n [0, threshhold] OpenMP will actually be slower than the serial algoirhtm due to the overhead B . Another important point is that OpenMP has a different overhead/threshold depending on if it has already been used in the code.  I call this the cold and warm thresholds. dtime_cold = omp_get_wtime();
foo();  //cold  - OpenMP has not been called before
dtime_cold = omp_get_wtime() - dtime_cold;

dtime_warm = omp_get_wtime();
foo(); //warm - OpenMP has already been called once
dtime_warm = omp_get_wtime() - dtime_warm; If n is large enough then the constant terms are insignficant in which case the thresholds don't matter.

